  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nThunderstorm potential returns Friday night\n\nFriday into Saturday, upper low churning over northern Saskatchewan \nshould usher a weak front south through the CWA. As this occurs, \ndisturbance within the quasi-zonal flow aloft over the Upper Midwest \nwill eject eastward, potentially (30-60%) leading to showers and a \nrumble of thunder. While shear appears supportive of severe \nthunderstorms, instability decidedly does not. Indeed, 10.00z LREF \njoint probabilities of sufficient shear, instability, and lack of \nCIN for severe storms are near zero. This is largely in line with \nthe overnight timing of the upper disturbance, right in the right \npart of the day to expect issues with instability. Thus, while \nincluding rain in the forecast is prudent, am not concerned about \nhazards aside from stray lightning.\n\nRainy Monday with flooding potential\n\nSunday into Monday, strong upper wave moves over the Rocky Mountains \nto the northern Dakotas as ridging continues to be present in the \nsoutheastern CONUS. With lee pressure falls occurring across the High \nPlains, this will induce robust southerly moisture advection from \nthe Gulf. By Sunday night, 10.00z LREF suggests PWAT values around \n1.6\" with potential (10%) for this to exceed 2\". 10.00z guidance \nsuggests precip will fall in 2 phases: 1) as WAA ramps up early \nMonday and a lead disturbance ejects northeastward over WI and 2) as \nthe cold front pushes southeast during the evening. Locations that \nsee moderate rain during both phases have a small chance (10-15%) to \nexceed 4\" in rainfall with extreme outcomes across the 10.00z \nGEFS/ECENS/CMCE having over 7\". Will need to monitor this closely, \nparticularly if guidance trends toward a less progressive pattern \nwith a lingering warm front, as those extreme outcomes would come \ninto play more.",
